    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d69">

                <pb facs="0191" n="95" sameAs="frus:frus1931-41v01/pg95"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94Commission/338</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 1656</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1932-08-02">August 2, 1932</date>.<lb/>[Received August
                        29.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: With reference to my telegram No. 883 of
                    August 1, 9 [<hi rendition="#italic">8</hi>] p.m.,<anchor n="14" xml:id="d69-f1"
                    /> I have the honor to enclose herewith in confidence a copy of a letter
                    addressed to the Chairman of the Commission of Study of the League of Nations
                    now investigating the causes of trouble between Japan and China by the Japanese
                        Assessor,<anchor n="15" xml:id="d69-f2"/> which encloses an epitomized
                    record of the statements which Count Uchida made at the interviews with that
                    Commission in Dairen and Tokyo. I was permitted to make copies of this document
                    for my confidential information, and I have the honor to request that the
                    Department treat the document as confidential.</p>
                <p>As stated in my telegram, my information is that while this document does not
                    accurately set forth all that Count Uchida said <pb facs="0192" n="96"
                        xml:id="pg96"/> to the Commission while it was in Tokyo, it is nevertheless
                    believed to be a clear and accurate statement of the policy which Count Uchida
                    as Minister of Foreign Affairs will follow in dealing with the Manchurian
                    situation.</p>
                <p>Respectfully yours,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Nelson Trusler
                    Johnson</hi></signed></closer>


                <xi:include href="d69-a1.xml" xpointer="d69-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
